0. Definitions.

"This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU Affero General Public License.

"Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds of
works, such as semiconductor masks.

"The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License. Each licensee is addressed as "you". "Licensees" and
"recipients" may be individuals or organizations.

To "modify" a work means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work
in a fashion requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an
exact copy. The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the
earlier work or a work "based on" the earlier work.

A "covered work" means either the unmodified Program or a work based
on the Program.

To "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
permission, would make you liable for infringement under applicable
copyright law, except executing it on a computer or modifying it
without distributing it. The threshold for "propagate" is making
any copies, or changing the work in a way that requires copyright
permission.

To "convey" a work means any kind of propagation that also enables
other parties to make copies of the work. Simple interaction with a user
is not "conveying". Interaction through a network does not count as
conveying if the user is not provided a copy of the work.

"Appropriate Legal Notices" means a display that is clearly visible to
the user of an interactive computer program, including a reasonable
opportunity to read it.

1. Source Code.

The "source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work
for making modifications to it. "Object code" means any non-source
form of a work.

A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for a particular kind of equipment or materials,
an interface that is widely used and accepted as standard.

The "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other
than the program's executable code, that (a) is included in the normal
form of packaging with such an executable, and (b) is not part of the
work's object code or its interface.

A "Corresponding Source" for a work in object code form means all
the source code needed to generate, install, and run the object code
and to perform the same function, excluding the System Libraries and
any incidental portions not essential to the object code itself.

